"### The Read \u2014 Q2 '26  \u00b7  WGC data as of 2026-06-30\n\n**Balance.** Supply ran **1,269 t** in Q2 '26 \u2014 mine 966 t, scrap 326 t, producer hedging -23 t \u2014 against **942 t** of measured demand across the five top-level segments. That leaves **+327 t**. Read it as the OTC-and-unreported balancing item, *not* a physical surplus: WGC's demand total excludes over-the-counter investment and stock movements, which is exactly what this residual absorbs.\n\n**What moved.** ETFs came in at **-45 t**, **-204 t (-128.2%)** against its own trailing four-quarter average of 159 t \u2014 the largest deviation of the five. For context this quarter: jewellery 310 t, technology 80 t, bars & coins 307 t, ETFs -45 t, central banks 289 t.\n\n**Central banks.** Officially reported reserves show central banks as net buyers of **+289 t** in the quarter. The trailing four quarters total **780 t**, against a five-year average pace of **907 t/yr** \u2014 running at **86%** of that pace.\n\n**ETFs.** Physically-backed funds shed **45 t** in the quarter (+419 t over the trailing four). ETF demand is the fastest-moving line in the stack and the one that flips sign most often \u2014 it has been negative in a large minority of quarters since 2010, which is why it appears below the axis in the mix chart.\n\n**Recycling.** Scrap supplied **326 t**, **25.7%** of total supply, versus a five-year average of 25.6%. Gold averaged **$4,506/oz** in the quarter, +37.4% year on year. Across the full 2010-to-date window the correlation between the year-on-year *change* in scrap's supply share and the year-on-year *change* in price is **+0.46** \u2014 scrap is the price-elastic part of the supply stack. In raw levels the same pair correlates only +0.01, because both series trend; the change-on-change reading is the one that carries information.\n\n<sub>All figures recomputed on every load from the WGC supply-and-demand feed. Nothing here is hardcoded.</sub>"